Response for query 94 after 3.25s:
This is an out-of-bounds read that manifests as an ASan SEGV in `uv_encode()`, called from `LogLuv24fromXYZ()`.

The crashing path is:

`LLVMFuzzerTestOneInput -> LogLuv24fromXYZ(custom_xyz, em) -> uv_encode(u, v, em)`

Inside `LogLuv24fromXYZ`, the fuzzer-controlled `custom_xyz` values are converted into chromaticity coordinates `u` and `v`. Then `uv_encode()` computes:

```c
vi = tiff_itrunc((v - UV_VSTART)*(1./UV_SQSIZ), em);
```

and immediately uses `vi` as an index into `uv_row`:

```c
if (u < uv_row[vi].ustart)
```

The bug is that `uv_encode()` only checks the upper bound:

```c
if (vi >= UV_NVS) return oog_encode(u, v);
```

but never checks whether `vi` is negative. Because the fuzzer fully controls the float inputs and also the `em` argument, `tiff_itrunc()` can return a negative `vi` even when `v >= UV_VSTART`, especially for malformed/NaN/extreme float values or unusual rounding behavior. That negative index causes `uv_row[vi]` to read before the array, leading to the invalid memory access.

So the root cause is missing lower-bound validation on the array index `vi` in `uv_encode()` before indexing `uv_row`.
